@@ -49,7 +49,7 @@
 - [CQRS](docs/cqrs.md): the opt-in, standalone `Rask.Cqrs` package — a source-generated, reflection-free CQRS/mediator. IQuery<T>/ICommand/ICommand<T>/IEvent + their handlers, dispatched via a single IDispatcher — Query for a query, Send for a command (response type inferred either way) and Publish for events; register with AddRaskCqrs(). Handlers are wired by a Roslyn generator (no runtime reflection, no assembly scanning, [DynamicDependency]-preserved ctors) so it publishes clean under the WASM/AOT trimmer. Pipeline behaviors (IPipelineBehavior<TRequest,TResult>, registered via AddOpenBehavior / AddBehavior) are the decorator hook for logging/transactions/caching; ONE ships — a ValidationBehavior registered outermost by AddRaskCqrs, which rejects an invalid request with RaskValidationException before its handler runs (400 problem+json with an errors object over remote dispatch). Events fan out Sequential or WhenAll. Diagnostics RASK028 (ambiguous handler) / RASK029 (unregisterable handler). Depends only on Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions. **Remote dispatch** — the companion `Rask.Cqrs.Client` / `Rask.Cqrs.Server` packages let a WASM-hosted app reach its server through the SAME IDispatcher call it makes in-process: no HttpClient at the call site, no /api/* endpoints to write, and nothing on a message marks it remote (you write a record and a handler, and where the project sits decides where it runs). One line each — AddRaskCqrsClient() in the browser, AddRaskCqrsServer() + MapRaskCqrs() on the host — and neither half references the other, so a browser bundle cannot compile the endpoint code and the server never carries the browser transport; put the messages where both halves compile them — a Shared project, or one file Compile-linked into the host — and the handlers in the server only, which is what keeps a connection string or a pricing rule out of a download anybody can read. A client is a PURE client: every request message it dispatches travels, so a handler sitting in the client project is BYPASSED and the server answers 404 for a name it has no handler for — [LocalOnly] is the only way to keep a message in-process, and it is what a browser-local counter or offline queue needs; events are the deliberate exception, fanning out locally AND travelling, exactly once each. The wire is TWO endpoints, not one per message — GET and POST on /_rask/cqrs/request/{name}, the verb carrying what IQuery and ICommand already declare, so a command is 405 on GET and cannot be triggered by a URL, a prefetch or a link scanner (a query too long for a URL falls back to POST with an identical result); the literal route segments outrank the dashboard's /_rask catch-all and the SPA fallback, the name being a route segment gives logs/metrics/rate-limit partitions the message name for free, and MapRaskCqrs() returns the endpoint group for .RequireRateLimiting(...)/CORS/output caching. It fails closed: authenticated-by-default with [AllowAnonymous] the only way past, [Authorize] supplying a policy AND roles (both enforced), an anonymous caller getting the same answer for a real message name as for a typo so the endpoint cannot be walked to enumerate the app's messages, both verbs requiring the X-Rask-Cqrs header no cross-site markup can set, and handler exceptions becoming RFC 9457 problem+json with no exception text unless IncludeExceptionDetail is opted into. Failure to ARRIVE is the one thing remote dispatch adds — RemoteDispatchException, whose null StatusCode means the request never reached the server. [LocalOnly] on an INTERFACE covers a whole family, which matters because IJob derives from ICommand — without it every job payload would become an internet-reachable endpoint. Mark DOMAIN EVENTS [LocalOnly] too: events travel from a browser like any message, so an unmarked OrderPlaced could be forged by a signed-in user. Files go both directions with no adapter at the call site: a message declares an IRaskFile directly and it uploads as multipart, a query returning FileDownload streams back with an attachment disposition, a safe-leaf filename and nosniff (Download.File(download) saves it); bounded by MaxUploadBytes/MaxFileCount, with chunked upload above ChunkedUploadThreshold, shipped in #764 — the server echoes X-Rask-Upload-Offset and answers 409 with its own offset on a mismatch, which the client reads and resumes from (#907, bounded per file). The two directions are NOT symmetric in the browser and the docs say which is which (#894): fetch has no request streaming, so a single-shot multipart upload costs its own size in the tab and ChunkedUploadThreshold is what bounds that — load-bearing, not a tuning knob; the download direction is streamed because the client asks for ResponseHeadersRead AND the WASM SDK defaults WasmEnableStreamingResponse to true, which the browser-rung publish gate now asserts on the shipped boot module because the default is the SDK's rather than Rask's. The two halves are put in front of EACH OTHER in tests/Rask.Cqrs.Transport.Tests — the only compilation that references both — and over a real browser fetch (#896); note that AddRaskCqrsClient() installs its remote invokers into the PROCESS-WIDE CqrsRegistry the endpoint also dispatches through, so one process cannot host both halves. The wire codecs are source-generated Utf8JsonWriter/Utf8JsonReader code, not reflection, so remote dispatch publishes clean under the WASM/AOT trimmer; RASK053 reports a shape with no wire encoding and reaches no existing code, because codecs are generated only for a compilation referencing one of the two transport packages. `rask new --template wasm-hosted` scaffolds the whole arrangement in one project (its server's Program.cs is `RaskApp.Create(args).Serve();`): the message records live in `Shared/`, which both halves compile; the browser app in `Client/` registers `AddRaskCqrsClient()` in its own `Client/Program.cs`; and `RaskClientPackageReference` (a reference the browser app gets and the server does not) is what keeps the two transports apart.
 - [Query](docs/query.md): the opt-in `Rask.Query` package — TanStack Query's model in C# over the CQRS dispatcher: request dedup, staleness (StaleTime 0 / GcTime 5 min), background refetch, retry, polling, keep-previous-data and prefix invalidation, one cache per live session (scoped; never process-wide). The static `QueryClient` reaches the session's cache with nothing injected, and a query follows its inputs in all three places it can be declared: in `Render` from current values (`var o = QueryClient.Query(new GetOrders(Page))` — the same call returns the same query every render, keyed by call site and occurrence so loops and `if`s are safe), in a `field ??=` property or the constructor from a lambda (`QueryClient.Query(() => new GetOrders(Page))`, re-run at every read, first run at the first read; returning null pauses it), or injected `IQueryClient` where there is no component. THE MESSAGE IS THE KEY (records compare structurally); `QueryKey.For<Person>(parts)` keys a function query over data with a type but no message, and a function query is handed the input its key was built from. Commands: `[Invalidates(typeof(GetOrders))]` on the command record, `QueryClient.Send(cmd)` (throws) or a renderable `QueryClient.Command<T>()` whose `Send` never throws (Status Idle/Pending/Success/Error with IsIdle/IsPending/IsSuccess/IsError, Error, `Variables` = the command last sent, `Data` on a value-returning command, `Reset()`; `key:` per row in a loop). OPTIMISTIC UPDATES ARE PER SEND, aimed at the query handle: `ship.Send(new ShipOrder(id)).Optimistically(orders.Optimistic(list => [.. list.Where(o => o.Id != id)]))` — shown at once, snapshotted before the send, replaced by the refetch on success, restored in reverse on failure; works for function queries and function commands too; a function command is `QueryClient.Command(invalidates: typeof(Person))` sent with `Send(ct => Person.Create(...))`. There is no SetMessage and no OnUpdated re-pointing. A RASK.DATA WRITE INVALIDATES BY ITSELF: once a save commits (after an explicit transaction's commit; never on rollback), every query about the types it wrote — `QueryKey.For<Person>(…)`, i.e. what `Invalidate<Person>()` reaches; a child entity counts as its aggregate — refetches in the session that made it (never another session, nothing outside a session). A message query like GetPeople still needs `[Invalidates(typeof(GetPeople))]` on the command. The hook is Rask.Data's scoped `IDataChanges`, which the Rask host implements with the session's IQueryClient — and which, IN A WEBASSEMBLY APP, Rask.Data's browser build wires itself from `AddRaskData<AppDbContext>()` (plus `AddRaskQuery()`): no `Db.Configure`, no `Db.UseScope`, no IDataChanges of your own.
 - [HTTP APIs](docs/api-endpoints.md): ordinary ASP.NET API controllers and minimal API endpoints, first-class. `Rask.Api` (AddRaskApi/MapRaskApi) maps controllers and — the actual defect — answers an UNMATCHED request under ApiOptions.Prefix (default /api) with an RFC 9457 404 instead of letting the catch-all render the app with a 200, which made a mistyped route fail as a JSON parse error far from its cause. Ordering is NOT the problem and never was: endpoint routing matches on precedence, so a route mapped after MapRask always ran. The guard is an ordinary endpoint at the default order, not a fallback (a fallback sits at int.MaxValue and loses to the catch-all). `Rask.Api.Client` generates one typed client per controller from the declaration itself — PostsController -> PostsClient, `await posts.Get(3)` with no URL, verb or response type restated — using the same reflection-free WireCodecEmitter as the CQRS wire; minimal APIs are read from the MapGet/MapPost invocations and grouped by route (/api/widgets -> WidgetsClient, .WithName overrides the derived method name), and TypedResults (Ok<T>, Results<Ok<T>, NotFound>, NoContent) carry the response type. Optional parameters keep the SERVER's default (emitting `= default` would send page=0 and override it), and the client sends Accept: application/json (a string action otherwise answers text/plain). RASK067-071 report what gets no client method and why. **Validated like everything else** (#988): `[Required]` and the discovered `AbstractValidator<T>` — async rules included — run on both a controller action and a minimal API, rejecting with the 400 documented at docs/validation.md#rejected, which `ApiException.Errors` surfaces. Choose this over CQRS when someone other than your own browser code has to call you.
-- [Web APIs from MDN](docs/web-apis.md): the `Rask.Web` package — every web API the browser ships, generated from MDN's data into C# (`using Rask.Web;`). MDN's globals by the window's own names (`Window`, `Navigator`, `Document`, `LocalStorage`, `Performance`, `Crypto`, …) and every interface they reach as a type in `Rask.Web.Types`; MDN's dictionaries and enums as records and enums. A chain is a path run in one round trip per `await` (`await Navigator.Clipboard.WriteText("hi")`, `await Window.MatchMedia(q).Matches`), an awaited object is kept as a disposable handle (`await using var q = await Window.MatchMedia(…)`), `Set{Name}` writes, `IsSupported` asks the browser. Bytes are `byte[]` both ways (`await Crypto.Subtle.Digest("SHA-256", bytes)`, `await Crypto.GetRandomValues(new byte[16])` answers with the filled array); MDN's `any` is your own type (`await channel.PostMessage(msg)` is `PostMessage<TMessage>`, `await response.Json<Order>()`, a MessageEvent's `e.Data<T>()`); a sequence of live objects is an array of kept objects, each disposed of on its own (`await Navigator.Usb.GetDevices()`, `Navigator.GetGamepads()` is `Gamepad?[]`); an argument MDN types as `object` takes the dictionary its page documents (`await Navigator.Permissions.Query(new() { Name = "geolocation" })`); a writable attribute holding an object takes one you kept, or null (`await Navigator.MediaSession.SetMetadata(await MediaMetadata.Create(new() { Title = "Song" }))`). A web API is generated once ONE engine ships it, so Chromium-only device APIs are there too (`Navigator.Usb`, `Navigator.Hid`, `Navigator.Bluetooth`, `Navigator.GetBattery()`, `Navigator.Connection`, `Navigator.Vibrate`, `EyeDropper`, `IdleDetector`), their doc comment opening with "Chromium only." — guard them with `IsSupported`; elements stay cross-engine. Call from a handler or OnRendered; nothing touching DOM nodes is generated (use a typed element ref); `new X(…)` is `await X.Create(…)` (kept), statics are `X.Member(…)`; events are `On{Event}` subscriptions to dispose of (`await Window.MatchMedia(q).OnChange(e => …)`, payload = MDN's event type), a callback argument is a C# handler and an element argument your ElementRef (`await using var io = await IntersectionObserver.Create(entries => …); await io.Observe(_card);`, `await Navigator.Locks.Request("x", async lk => …)` holds the lock until the handler returns); what a callback hands you that is only data (IntersectionObserverEntry, ResizeObserverEntry, MutationRecord, Lock) is a record under MDN's name with MDN's nullability (`e.IsIntersecting` is a bool); either must belong to a component (it re-renders it), and disposing of an observer drops its handlers. Tests fake any web object with `Fake()` (`using var c = Navigator.Clipboard.Fake(); c.Returns(x => x.ReadText(), "hi"); c.Calls; c.Raise("change", e)`), scoped to the test's flow. HOSTS: what needs the user's click in progress (Navigator.Share, Notification.RequestPermission, MediaDevices.GetDisplayMedia, ScreenOrientation.Lock, PaymentRequest.Show; element refs' RequestFullscreen/RequestPointerLock/ShowPicker) and the per-frame families (WebGL + its OES_/EXT_/WEBGL_ extension objects, WebGPU, AudioWorklet) are generated into Rask.Wasm ONLY, as C# 14 extension members of Rask.Web's types (`WasmMembers`, `WasmElementRefMembers`) — the same call in a WASM app, a compile error in a server app. The globals are sealed classes (not static) so they can be extended. Doc comments say "Secure contexts only" from MDN's [SecureContext].
+- [Web APIs from MDN](docs/web-apis.md): the `Rask.Web` package — every web API the browser ships, generated from MDN's data into C#, imported for you in every Server and WASM app (the package adds `global using Rask.Web;` plus the alias `global using Types = Rask.Web.Types;`, so `private Types.IntersectionObserver? _io;`; `Rask.Web.Types` itself is never imported — its interfaces share the globals' names; a library referencing `Rask` alone gets neither; where a global is named like a Rask type, Rask's keeps the bare name by global alias — `EditContext`, `FormData`, `DataTransfer`, `EventTarget`, `Touch` are Rask's; MDN's are `Rask.Web.FormData`, `Types.EditContext`). MDN's globals by the window's own names (`Window`, `Navigator`, `Document`, `LocalStorage`, `Performance`, `Crypto`, …) and every interface they reach as a type in `Rask.Web.Types`; MDN's dictionaries and enums as records and enums. A chain is a path run in one round trip per `await` (`await Navigator.Clipboard.WriteText("hi")`, `await Window.MatchMedia(q).Matches`), an awaited object is kept as a disposable handle (`await using var q = await Window.MatchMedia(…)`), `Set{Name}` writes, `IsSupported` asks the browser. Bytes are `byte[]` both ways (`await Crypto.Subtle.Digest("SHA-256", bytes)`, `await Crypto.GetRandomValues(new byte[16])` answers with the filled array); MDN's `any` is your own type (`await channel.PostMessage(msg)` is `PostMessage<TMessage>`, `await response.Json<Order>()`, a MessageEvent's `e.Data<T>()`); a sequence of live objects is an array of kept objects, each disposed of on its own (`await Navigator.Usb.GetDevices()`, `Navigator.GetGamepads()` is `Gamepad?[]`); an argument MDN types as `object` takes the dictionary its page documents (`await Navigator.Permissions.Query(new() { Name = "geolocation" })`); a writable attribute holding an object takes one you kept, or null (`await Navigator.MediaSession.SetMetadata(await MediaMetadata.Create(new() { Title = "Song" }))`). A web API is generated once ONE engine ships it, so Chromium-only device APIs are there too (`Navigator.Usb`, `Navigator.Hid`, `Navigator.Bluetooth`, `Navigator.GetBattery()`, `Navigator.Connection`, `Navigator.Vibrate`, `EyeDropper`, `IdleDetector`), their doc comment opening with "Chromium only." — guard them with `IsSupported`; elements stay cross-engine. Call from a handler or OnRendered; nothing touching DOM nodes is generated (use a typed element ref); `new X(…)` is `await X.Create(…)` (kept), statics are `X.Member(…)`; events are `On{Event}` subscriptions to dispose of (`await Window.MatchMedia(q).OnChange(e => …)`, payload = MDN's event type), a callback argument is a C# handler and an element argument your ElementRef (`await using var io = await IntersectionObserver.Create(entries => …); await io.Observe(_card);`, `await Navigator.Locks.Request("x", async lk => …)` holds the lock until the handler returns); what a callback hands you that is only data (IntersectionObserverEntry, ResizeObserverEntry, MutationRecord, Lock) is a record under MDN's name with MDN's nullability (`e.IsIntersecting` is a bool); either must belong to a component (it re-renders it), and disposing of an observer drops its handlers. Tests fake any web object with `Fake()` (`using var c = Navigator.Clipboard.Fake(); c.Returns(x => x.ReadText(), "hi"); c.Calls; c.Raise("change", e)`), scoped to the test's flow. HOSTS: what needs the user's click in progress (Navigator.Share, Notification.RequestPermission, MediaDevices.GetDisplayMedia, ScreenOrientation.Lock, PaymentRequest.Show; element refs' RequestFullscreen/RequestPointerLock/ShowPicker) and the per-frame families (WebGL + its OES_/EXT_/WEBGL_ extension objects, WebGPU, AudioWorklet) are generated into Rask.Wasm ONLY, as C# 14 extension members of Rask.Web's types (`WasmMembers`, `WasmElementRefMembers`) — the same call in a WASM app, a compile error in a server app. The globals are sealed classes (not static) so they can be extended. Doc comments say "Secure contexts only" from MDN's [SecureContext].
 - [Browser APIs](docs/browser-apis.md): the map of the typed Web-API wrappers (17: 13 shared + 4 WASM-only; everything else the browser ships is MDN's own surface in Rask.Web) — shared vs WASM-only, one-shot vs subscription, with the inject-from-ctor pattern and the push/[JSInvokable] mechanism. Every element component and every wrapper carries an XML `<summary>` that names its MDN reference page, and the generator forwards it (plus a `<param>` per documented property) onto the chain's entry and steps, so the documentation appears at the CALL SITE — hovering `Video` — not only on the type. BACKGROUND SYNC: `IBackgroundSync` (WASM only) wraps SyncManager + PeriodicSyncManager. The boundary is the whole design: the browser fires the sync even with the tab CLOSED, but the .NET runtime lives in the PAGE, not the service worker, so C# runs only while a client is open — Rask SW forwards the tag to every open client and the registration is otherwise consumed unseen. Hence: re-request tags at boot, keep the work in IIndexedDb/OPFS and let the sync be the NUDGE to drain it, and expect the win on a backgrounded tab rather than a closed one. Its SW handler deliberately stays out of rask-sw-shared.ts so the Server SW does not advertise a capability that cannot fire there. Periodic sync has no request API — check GetPeriodicPermissionAsync, do not ask. ANIMATION, SPEECH, FULLSCREEN, PICTURE-IN-PICTURE: no wrappers — Rask.Web (docs/web-apis.md): `await using var fade = await _box.Animate([new() { ["opacity"] = 0 }, new() { ["opacity"] = 1 }], 300); await fade.Finished;`, `await using var u = await SpeechSynthesisUtterance.Create("Hello"); await SpeechSynthesis.Speak(u);`, and on WASM `await _stage.RequestFullscreen()` / `await Document.ExitFullscreen()` and `await using var pip = await _video.RequestPictureInPicture()`; `await Document.FullscreenElement == _stage` says whether it is yours. Reduced motion is the APP's call for its own animations. VIEW TRANSITIONS: `IViewTransitions` is the one wrapper an app could not have written itself — a same-document transition must WRAP the DOM mutation, and the mutation is the framework's morph, so enabling it routes the live runtime's own commit (diff and full-document, both hosts) through `document.startViewTransition`. Off by default, and off is byte-for-byte the previous timing; `prefers-reduced-motion` is honoured in the runtime because the animation is the browser's own default with no stylesheet of ours to switch off. REALTIME: `IWebRtc` (peer connections + data channels, every host), `IMediaStreams` (a MediaStream can't cross interop, so the framework holds it under a MediaStreamId and attaches it to a `<video>`), and `ISignaling` + the opt-in standalone `Rask.Signaling` package on the server (`AddRaskSignaling()` + `MapRaskSignaling()`, which needs `UseWebSockets()` or every upgrade 400s). The relay authenticates by default, mints peer ids server-side (never trusting the client's), delivers only within the sender's own room, and treats the payload as an opaque string end to end — it never parses an SDP or an ICE candidate.
 - [JS interop](docs/js-interop.md): scoped CSS/TypeScript (a `.js` sibling is RASK055; tsgo compiles it, no npm) — the owning component calls each `export function` (or arrow in an `export const`)/`export class` of its `.ts` as a typed private method (`await Width(_box)`, `NewChart(el)`; callbacks as sync/async overloads, interfaces as nested records, a whole tuple `[x: number, y: string]` as `(double X, string Y)`, `number` is always `double`; an export with no C# shape is RASK094), element refs (typed to the MDN interface they carry its generated DOM members — `new ElementRef<HTMLDialogElement>()`, `await _dialog.ShowModal()`, `await _dialog.Open`, no IJSRuntime or Async suffix), IJSRuntime, typed browser APIs — shared (both transports) in Rask.Core.Browser (ICookies/ISpeechRecognition/IStorageEstimator/IDeviceOrientation/IDeviceMotion/IIndexedDb/IWebAuthn/IWebLocks/IMediaStreams/ISignaling/IWebRtc/IViewTransitions plus the PWA APIs IWebPush/IWakeLock — storage, clipboard, geolocation, matchMedia, screen, battery, network, vibration, performance, page visibility, crypto, permissions, BroadcastChannel, media session, speech synthesis (SpeechSynthesis.Speak), animations (an element ref's Animate), the observers, gamepads (Navigator.GetGamepads), file pickers (Window.ShowOpenFilePicker, WASM), the origin private file system (Navigator.Storage.GetDirectory), notifications (Notification.Create) and the app badge (Navigator.SetAppBadge) are MDN's own surface in Rask.Web (docs/web-apis.md), not wrappers — and the all-host headless Shareable — OS share sheet, attaches data-rask-share to your element and fires navigator.share in the click gesture so it works on Server too, plus the all-host Trigger.Gesture family — Trigger.Fullscreen/Trigger.ScreenOrientation/Trigger.EyeDropper/Trigger.Install/Trigger.MediaCapture/Trigger.PictureInPicture, headless data-rask-gesture components that run activation-gated WASM-only APIs inside the click gesture so they reach the Server host declaratively); WASM-only in Rask.Wasm.Browser (IInstallPrompt/IBackgroundSync + IMediaDevices; code-driven share, RequestFullscreen()/RequestPictureInPicture() on an element ref, orientation lock, EyeDropper, IdleDetector permission, WebUSB (Navigator.Usb), WebHID (Navigator.Hid), Web Serial (Navigator.Serial.RequestPort) and Web Bluetooth (Navigator.Bluetooth.RequestDevice) are Rask.Web members generated into Rask.Wasm only).
 - [AOT compilation](docs/aot.md): opt-in full WASM AOT via `-p:RaskWasmAot=true` (default keeps the Mono interpreter; needs the wasm-tools workload). A reflection-free `TypedParserRegistry` seeds every BCL `IParsable` primitive and resolves route/query/form string values with no runtime generics; the RoutesGenerator auto-registers custom `IParsable` route/query param types; `RaskBinding.RegisterParsable<T>()` registers custom form-model value types; `InvokeAsync<T>` needs a `JsonSerializerContext` for custom T. Runtime builds under `IsAotCompatible` and the WASM sample under `EnableAotAnalyzer`, so every warnings-as-errors build enforces AOT-safety; the full AOT publish runs nightly. Mono WASM AOT is mixed-mode (interpreter stays), so the win is a clean bundle + faster binding, not crash-avoidance.
